    "description": "Northstar Health needs a shared credential vault used by the billing service, the reporting service, and several scheduled jobs. Today each caller retrieves a signing key, decrypts the value itself, and performs its own role check, so authorization logic is duplicated and the batch exporter can bypass checks introduced elsewhere. The Vault API team will create a KeyStore component that retains all key material and connection handles internally. Its published operations will include Sign, Decrypt, and Rotate only; no method will return raw bytes, key identifiers with embedded secrets, or the underlying provider client. Callers will provide the subject, requested operation, and resource scope, and the component will consult the existing AccessRules service before acting. Revocation and tenant changes must take effect on the next operation, including calls from retries and recovery workers. The migration may wrap legacy provider clients, but direct provider credentials must never be distributed to application code or retained in process memory beyond the vault boundary.",
    "decision_statement": "Decide whether all credential use should be confined behind a single owning KeyStore component instead of exposing key material and distributing authorization checks across callers.",
    "notes": "The KeyStore owns all key material and exposes only checked Sign/Decrypt/Rotate operations (protected-system); complete-mediation is the near-miss via the next-operation revocation rule on every path, with policy-guard supporting via the external AccessRules consultation."
  },
